<template name="window_shell" content="content">
<head>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="window_shell.rcss"/>
</head>
<!-- Legacy theme's shared window shell -- the msgbox sprite-frame chrome (top/middle/bottom cap
     composite + optional title-banner swap), the same asset/mechanism generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss
     uses for its own back-fill + cap composite. Not dialog-specific: any window needing this same
     chrome can link it (window_shell_item is a separate, unbuilt sibling for the 5-piece
     item-window composite family; legacy's login/sys_menu pair stay bespoke, too little shared
     structure to templatize).

     A consumer document links this file as a template resource in its own head section and sets
     this template's name as its own body element's template attribute (see
     generic_menu_dialog.rml for the worked example) -- whatever content that consumer's body
     element contains gets spliced into #content below.

     DO NOT write a literal angle-bracket example of a body-with-template-attribute tag anywhere in
     this file, comments included -- RmlUi's own Template::Load() (Source/Core/Template.cpp) finds
     this template's real head and body regions via a raw, comment-blind text search for the first
     literal opening-bracket-plus-word match, not real XML parsing. A stray example that merely
     LOOKS like a body tag with this same template's own name as its template attribute value gets
     sliced in as if it were real markup, self-referentially injecting this template into itself and
     recursing until a stack overflow.

     ALSO avoid writing "head" and "body" separated only by a slash (as in the two words joined by
     a plain forward slash, no spaces) anywhere in this file, comments included -- RmlUi's own
     FindTag() (Source/Core/XMLParseTools.cpp) tracks whether the found word was preceded by a slash
     in a `found_closing` flag that is declared OUTSIDE its own per-candidate search loop and never
     reset between rejected candidates within one call. A slash sitting directly before either word
     anywhere in this file flips that flag for the rest of that same search, so every later
     candidate -- including the one real body tag below -- gets compared against the wrong
     open/close expectation and rejected, and the template silently fails to load.

     Requires from the consumer's own data model: `has_title` (bool) and `title` (string). Also
     requires `positioned` (bool) and `root_x`/`root_y` (real device-pixel numbers, NOT reference-
     resolution values needing a scale transform -- this template's own dp chrome already assumes
     no resolution-based rescaling; a consumer that also needs UI::Scaling's older
     reference-resolution convention doesn't belong on this template -- a `root_scale`+
     `transform:scale()` variant would double-scale this shell's own already-real-sized chrome).
     Every consumer that never sets `positioned` true
     keeps today's plain screen-centered behavior unchanged (an empty-string style value leaves the
     `.center-both` class's own left/top in effect, since an inline style only overrides when it
     actually holds a value) -- `option_window.rml` is the first consumer to opt in, for a window
     that can be dragged to a specific real-pixel spot instead of always centering.
     `#window_shell_header` (the header rail, given a stable id here rather than only a class) is
     the drag handle a positioned consumer can pass to `UI::RmlBridge::MakeDraggable()`, same
     pattern as `my_inventory.rml`'s `#title` -- `CGenericMenuDialog` doesn't do this today, native
     menu dialogs were never draggable.

     id="panel" set here, not by the consumer -- every current *_dialog.rcss defines its own
     document-scoped `#panel { width/height/... }` sizing rule (RmlUi CSS/IDs are per-document, so
     reusing the same id across different documents' own window_shell instances is safe), the same
     way the pre-template markup always named this element #panel.

     `.center-both` (base.rcss) also sets `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`, on top of its own
     left:50%/top:50% -- that class stays applied even in positioned mode (only left/top get an
     inline override above), so a positioned consumer's root_x/root_y (an already-final real-pixel
     top-left, not a center point) would otherwise still get shifted by an extra half the panel's
     own width/height. .window-shell-positioned (window_shell.rcss) cancels it via a toggled class,
     not a raw data-style-transform override -- an inline style set to the empty string (the
     ternary's false branch) is safe for left/top (a plain length that just fails to parse and
     falls through to the cascade), but for transform specifically an empty-string override instead
     renders this panel full-screen rather than just losing the -50%/-50% shift.
     data-class-* is the same proven toggle mechanism every other
     conditional class here already uses (data-class-hidden, data-class-checked, etc). -->
<body id="panel" class="center-both"
      data-style-left="positioned ? (root_x + 'px') : ''"
      data-style-top="positioned ? (root_y + 'px') : ''"
      data-class-window-shell-positioned="positioned">
	<div class="window-shell-sprite-back"></div>
	<div class="window-shell-sprite-top"></div>
	<div class="window-shell-sprite-middle"></div>
	<div class="window-shell-sprite-bottom"></div>
	<div id="window_shell_header" class="window-shell-header-rail" data-class-hidden="!has_title"></div>
	<div class="window-shell-header-title" data-class-hidden="!has_title">{{title}}</div>
	<div id="content" class="window-shell-content" data-class-has-title="has_title"></div>
	<!-- Optional footer anchor, sibling of #content -- a direct #panel child, same relationship
	     generic_confirm_dialog.rcss's own .gcd-btn-primary/etc have to #panel (not nested inside
	     .gcd-body). #content is a single splice target (Template::ParseTemplate only resolves one
	     content id), so a consumer can't author an element here directly; empty and inert by
	     default (harmless for generic_menu_dialog, which never touches it) until a consumer builds
	     into it in C++ (Rml::ElementDocument::CreateElement() + AppendChild(), NOT authored as
	     `{{}}`-bound RML markup elsewhere and moved here after load -- that was tried first
	     (option_window.rml's close button) and never rendered its label text: RmlUi's
	     text-interpolation DataView is only created at an element's original XML parse, and
	     re-attaching to a new parent doesn't recreate it, only re-scans attributes for data-*
	     bindings) -- see COptionWindow::BuildRmlUi() for the current worked example. -->
	<div id="window_shell_footer" class="window-shell-footer"></div>
</body>
</template>
